{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011WLPddWB2bkpacQ93cUGV3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/core_concepts/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-31T06:15:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Argo project (Argo CD; CNCF graduated)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D3.1\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"argo-cd\", \"argo-cd-application-resource\", \"source-of-truth\", \"manifest-source\", \"synced-outofsync\", \"sync-operation\", \"drift-detection\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Argo CD — Core Concepts / Glossary\n9\t\n10\tQuoted entries from the page's glossary:\n11\t\n12\t**Application:** \"A group of Kubernetes resources as defined by a manifest. This is a Custom Resource Definition (CRD).\"\n13\t\n14\t**Application source type:** \"Which Tool is used to build the application.\"\n15\t\n16\t**Target state:** \"The desired state of an application, as represented by files in a Git repository.\"\n17\t\n18\t**Live state:** \"The live state of that application. What pods etc are deployed.\"\n19\t\n20\t**Sync status:** \"Whether or not the live state matches the target state. Is the deployed application the same as Git says it should be?\"\n21\t\n22\t**Sync:** \"The process of making an application move to its target state. E.g. by applying changes to a Kubernetes cluster.\"\n23\t\n24\t**Sync operation status:** \"Whether or not a sync succeeded.\"\n25\t\n26\t**Refresh:** \"Compare the latest code in Git with the live state. Figure out what is different.\"\n27\t\n28\t**Health:** \"The health of the application, is it running correctly? Can it serve requests?\"\n29\t\n30\t**Tool:** \"A tool to create manifests from a directory of files. E.g. Kustomize. See Application Source Type.\"\n31\t\n32\t**Configuration management tool:** \"See Tool.\"\n33\t\n34\t**Configuration management plugin:** \"A custom tool.\"\n35\t\n36\tCAPTURE NOTE: this page carries no entry for Project or AppProject. Those are\n37\tdefined on the declarative-setup page — see argocd-declarative-setup-2026-08-31.md.\n38\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b05eb31d-1c28-48b6-bc35-59f5a9d9b150","uuid":"35f38580-b49c-434a-b23f-90ab6e42c301","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:20:04.977Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/argocd-core-concepts-2026-08-31.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/core_concepts/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-31T06:15:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Argo project (Argo CD; CNCF graduated)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D3.1\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"argo-cd\", \"argo-cd-application-resource\", \"source-of-truth\", \"manifest-source\", \"synced-outofsync\", \"sync-operation\", \"drift-detection\"]\n---\n# Argo CD — Core Concepts / Glossary\n\nQuoted entries from the page's glossary:\n\n**Application:** \"A group of Kubernetes resources as defined by a manifest. This is a Custom Resource Definition (CRD).\"\n\n**Application source type:** \"Which Tool is used to build the application.\"\n\n**Target state:** \"The desired state of an application, as represented by files in a Git repository.\"\n\n**Live state:** \"The live state of that application. What pods etc are deployed.\"\n\n**Sync status:** \"Whether or not the live state matches the target state. Is the deployed application the same as Git says it should be?\"\n\n**Sync:** \"The process of making an application move to its target state. E.g. by applying changes to a Kubernetes cluster.\"\n\n**Sync operation status:** \"Whether or not a sync succeeded.\"\n\n**Refresh:** \"Compare the latest code in Git with the live state. Figure out what is different.\"\n\n**Health:** \"The health of the application, is it running correctly? Can it serve requests?\"\n\n**Tool:** \"A tool to create manifests from a directory of files. E.g. Kustomize. See Application Source Type.\"\n\n**Configuration management tool:** \"See Tool.\"\n\n**Configuration management plugin:** \"A custom tool.\"\n\nCAPTURE NOTE: this page carries no entry for Project or AppProject. Those are\ndefined on the declarative-setup page — see argocd-declarative-setup-2026-08-31.md.\n","numLines":38,"startLine":1,"totalLines":38}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019Kji7HcNDMDTsmG6M2r9AM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://glossary.cncf.io/gitops/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-31T06:15:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF Cloud Native Glossary)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D3.1\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"gitops\", \"drift-detection\", \"self-heal\", \"rollback-by-revert\", \"source-of-truth\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# CNCF Glossary — GitOps\n9\t\n10\t## What it is\n11\t\"GitOps is a set of practices for managing software applications and infrastructure by continuously evaluating and reconciling their desired states as defined in a version control system such as Git against their actual state.\"\n12\t\n13\t## Problem it addresses\n14\tNamed problems: \"configuration drift, failed deployments, inconsistent environments, deployment failures, and difficulty tracking historical changes.\"\n15\t\n16\t\"Configuration drift can be hard to detect and resolve without a source of truth governing it.\"\n17\t\n18\t## How it helps\n19\tGitOps manages \"the entire infrastructure, application development, and deployment lifecycle using a single and unified process.\"\n20\t\n21\tNamed benefits: \"transparency and traceability of changes, reliability and security through declarative states, and rollback, revert, and self-healing attributes.\"\n22\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b05eb31d-1c28-48b6-bc35-59f5a9d9b150","uuid":"8304aea9-7367-4f5d-8f29-c545a713eab8","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:20:04.978Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/cncf-glossary-gitops-2026-08-31.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://glossary.cncf.io/gitops/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-31T06:15:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF Cloud Native Glossary)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D3.1\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"gitops\", \"drift-detection\", \"self-heal\", \"rollback-by-revert\", \"source-of-truth\"]\n---\n# CNCF Glossary — GitOps\n\n## What it is\n\"GitOps is a set of practices for managing software applications and infrastructure by continuously evaluating and reconciling their desired states as defined in a version control system such as Git against their actual state.\"\n\n## Problem it addresses\nNamed problems: \"configuration drift, failed deployments, inconsistent environments, deployment failures, and difficulty tracking historical changes.\"\n\n\"Configuration drift can be hard to detect and resolve without a source of truth governing it.\"\n\n## How it helps\nGitOps manages \"the entire infrastructure, application development, and deployment lifecycle using a single and unified process.\"\n\nNamed benefits: \"transparency and traceability of changes, reliability and security through declarative states, and rollback, revert, and self-healing attributes.\"\n","numLines":22,"startLine":1,"totalLines":22}}}
