{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LtbKPpj5PPkF2Mp8xgNWS9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tI have both binding contracts, the arc-outline block, B1's D3.1 concept and trap tables, B4's budget, the five cached D3.1 snapshots, and every published cross-bearing into Ch 15 verified by line number against chapters 01–14. Three findings shape this outline: **four section numbers are pinned by shipped text** (§3, §4, §5, §7), **Argo CD's first appearance in the book is Chapter 3, not this chapter**, and **the two sections carrying the chapter's most specific promises — §4's agent identity and §5's sync ordering — have no cached source at all**.\n2\t\n3\t```\n4\t---\n5\tchapter: 15\n6\tchapter_type: \"content\"\n7\ttitle: \"The Chart Is the Truth\"\n8\tsubtitle: \"GitOps is the control loop you already learned, pointed at a repository\"\n9\texam_domain: \"Cloud Native Application Delivery (competency: Application Delivery)\"\n10\tdomain_weight_pct: 7\n11\tcomplexity: \"mixed\"\n12\tnovelty: \"paradigm-shifting\"\n13\tprereq_factor: \"heavy\"\n14\t\n15\t#-- SUBTITLE NOTE. Eleven words, from the arc outline and the chapter lineup,\n16\t#-- carried forward unmodified. It is one word over the ≤10 guideline; the\n17\t#-- overrun is accepted because this string is quoted verbatim in two upstream\n18\t#-- contracts and because it IS the §7 Zenith claim. Subtitle and synthesis\n19\t#-- therefore agree by construction (the Ch 12 / Ch 13 / Ch 14 pattern).\n20\t#--\n21\t#-- ⚠ But note what that costs, because it is unusual: this subtitle states\n22\t#-- the chapter's PRIMARY ZENITH on the chapter's second line. Ch 14's\n23\t#-- subtitle did the same for a smaller payoff. The consequence is that §7\n24\t#-- cannot rely on surprise — it must earn the recognition by DEMONSTRATION,\n25\t#-- having announced the claim 25 pages earlier. Drafting should treat the\n26\t#-- subtitle as a promissory note the chapter spends seven sections redeeming,\n27\t#-- not as a spoiler to work around. See Open Question 6.\n28\t\n29\t#-- EXAM_DOMAIN NOTE. D3.1 Application Delivery, in the house form shipped by\n30\t#-- ch-04/-09/-10/-11/-12/-13/-14. The published domain weight is 16%\n31\t#-- [source: cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23]; the metadata line states\n32\t#-- that figure with its tag, followed by the house disclaimer that the split\n33\t#-- across Ch 14-16 is an authored allocation (B1 gap G33, B2 disclosure #1).\n34\t#-- Do NOT present 7% as published.\n35\t#--\n36\t#-- ⚠ HONESTY CONSTRAINT — inherited from Ch 14, and SHARPER here.\n37\t#-- Verified 2026-08-31 against cncf-kcna-curriculum-pdf-2026-08-23 (line 15):\n38\t#-- CNCF publishes the competency name \"Application Delivery\" and nothing\n39\t#-- else. The snapshot contains no occurrence of \"GitOps\", \"Argo\", \"Flux\",\n40\t#-- \"Helm\", \"twelve-factor\", or \"canary\". This chapter's ENTIRE topic list is\n41\t#-- authored inference. Unlike Ch 14, there is not even an LFS250 syllabus\n42\t#-- snapshot to lean on — no cached source enumerates that course's modules.\n43\t#-- What the corpus DOES support: Argo and Flux are both CNCF **graduated**\n44\t#-- projects [source: cncf-project-maturity-levels-2026-08-23] and OpenGitOps\n45\t#-- is a CNCF project [source: opengitops-principles-2026-08-23]. That is the\n46\t#-- honest basis for the inference and it should be the basis stated.\n47\t#-- Binding consequences:\n48\t#--   * Ch 14 already made this beat at length in Why This Chapter Matters.\n49\t#--     DO NOT repeat it at length — that is channel redundancy (skill Part 7).\n50\t#--     One short back-bearing to Ch 14's statement, then move on.\n51\t#--   * No GitOps / Argo CD / Flux / strategy fact may be framed as\n52\t#--     \"frequently tested\", \"commonly appears\", or any frequency claim.\n53\t#--     Skill Part 14, Ethical Guardrail #8.\n54\t#--   * B1 traps 73-81 are [source]-tagged and are real confusions, so they\n55\t#--     may be called \"easy to confuse.\" They may NOT be called common exam\n56\t#--     material. The tag licenses the confusion, not the frequency.\n57\t#-- See Open Question 7.\n58\t\n59\t#-- NOVELTY NOTE. `paradigm-shifting`, and this is a considered call rather\n60\t#-- than the default. B1 trap 74 — \"missing 'pulled', assuming a pipeline"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b05eb31d-1c28-48b6-bc35-59f5a9d9b150","uuid":"8cb6171d-33c7-4ebb-b782-4138199a1e0b","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:20:09.682Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/.pipeline-state/ch-15/outline.md","content":"I have both binding contracts, the arc-outline block, B1's D3.1 concept and trap tables, B4's budget, the five cached D3.1 snapshots, and every published cross-bearing into Ch 15 verified by line number against chapters 01–14. Three findings shape this outline: **four section numbers are pinned by shipped text** (§3, §4, §5, §7), **Argo CD's first appearance in the book is Chapter 3, not this chapter**, and **the two sections carrying the chapter's most specific promises — §4's agent identity and §5's sync ordering — have no cached source at all**.\n\n```\n---\nchapter: 15\nchapter_type: \"content\"\ntitle: \"The Chart Is the Truth\"\nsubtitle: \"GitOps is the control loop you already learned, pointed at a repository\"\nexam_domain: \"Cloud Native Application Delivery (competency: Application Delivery)\"\ndomain_weight_pct: 7\ncomplexity: \"mixed\"\nnovelty: \"paradigm-shifting\"\nprereq_factor: \"heavy\"\n\n#-- SUBTITLE NOTE. Eleven words, from the arc outline and the chapter lineup,\n#-- carried forward unmodified. It is one word over the ≤10 guideline; the\n#-- overrun is accepted because this string is quoted verbatim in two upstream\n#-- contracts and because it IS the §7 Zenith claim. Subtitle and synthesis\n#-- therefore agree by construction (the Ch 12 / Ch 13 / Ch 14 pattern).\n#--\n#-- ⚠ But note what that costs, because it is unusual: this subtitle s