{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P9H81cydVAKppWFQEcZVCy","type":"tool_result","content":"387:## ⚪ §1 — Why a Folder of YAML Stops Working\n1387:And it has bought you less than it should have, because the unit still gets applied by a person. Somebody with cluster credentials on their laptop, running a command, from a machine nobody audits, at a moment nobody records. They apply the version they believe is current. Afterward, nothing keeps watch. If someone edits an object by hand next Tuesday, the cluster and the package quietly stop agreeing, and no one finds out until the next deploy overwrites the fix or the fix overwrites the deploy.\n1393:> *\"The chart is what you meant. The cluster is what happened. The interesting question is who keeps them the same.\"*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b05eb31d-1c28-48b6-bc35-59f5a9d9b150","uuid":"2ee367d8-99f0-4327-b16f-631fb1d58a18","timestamp":"2026-08-31T11:20:37.419Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"387:## ⚪ §1 — Why a Folder of YAML Stops Working\n1387:And it has bought you less than it should have, because the unit still gets applied by a person. Somebody with cluster credentials on their laptop, running a command, from a machine nobody audits, at a moment nobody records. They apply the version they believe is current. Afterward, nothing keeps watch. If someone edits an object by hand next Tuesday, the cluster and the package quietly stop agreeing, and no one finds out until the next deploy overwrites the fix or the fix overwrites the deploy.\n1393:> *\"The chart is what you meant. The cluster is what happened. The interesting question is who keeps them the same.\"*","numLines":3,"totalLines":3}}
